# QSM Desktop Agent

This package is installed **inside a Linux VM**, not on Proxmox.

1. Enable QSM Display1 in VM Hardware -> Display and restart the VM. This
   creates `org.qsm.direct.agent` on virtio-serial.
2. Install the package in the guest: `sudo apt install ./qsm-desktop-agent_*.deb`.
3. Select the desktop user once. This enables the per-user system agent and
   its Wayland clipboard bridge; if the VM was not restarted after step 1 it
   will start automatically when the virtio port next appears:
   `sudo qsm-desktop-agent-setup USER`.
4. Log in to that user's Wayland desktop. The bridge waits harmlessly for the
   compositor when the VM is at SDDM, and becomes ready as soon as the desktop
   creates its Wayland socket. No second setup command is required.

The Desktop Agent complements the standard `qemu-guest-agent`; it handles only
the logged-in desktop's plain UTF-8 clipboard (up to 1 MiB). It never exposes
the guest filesystem. `wl-clipboard` is required for the Wayland clipboard
bridge; install it with `sudo apt install wl-clipboard`.

On KDE Plasma Wayland, the bridge uses Klipper's user-session D-Bus interface
and inotify notifications. It does not poll the Wayland clipboard while idle,
so no clipboard indicator or compositor repaint is generated without an
actual copy/paste event. Other Wayland compositors retain the portable,
low-frequency `wl-clipboard` fallback.

## macOS guest

The same agent and QSF1 wire protocol build for a macOS guest with
`packaging/guest/build-qsm-desktop-agent-macos.sh` (needs the Xcode command
line tools: `clang`, Foundation and AppKit). macOS has a single, synchronous,
system-wide clipboard, so the macOS build talks to `NSPasteboard` directly
(`guest/qsm_macos_clipboard.m`) and needs none of the Linux state file, Wayland
bridge, `wl-clipboard`, or inotify: CLIP_SET applies at once and EVENT_CLIP is
driven by the pasteboard `changeCount`. Only plain UTF-8 text is carried.

macOS exposes the QEMU virtio-serial port by its name as a callout serial
device — `/dev/cu.org.qsm.direct.agent`, not Linux's `/dev/virtio-ports/...` —
so no driver is needed on macOS 26. That path is the agent's default there.

Install it in the macOS guest (verified on macOS 26.1, VMware SVGA + QSM CPU
Display1):

```sh
# In the guest, after building qsm-desktop-agent:
sudo install -m 0755 qsm-desktop-agent /usr/local/bin/qsm-desktop-agent
cp org.qsm.desktop-agent.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.qsm.desktop-agent.plist
```

The LaunchAgent (`packaging/guest/org.qsm.desktop-agent.plist`) must live in
`~/Library/LaunchAgents` so it runs inside the user's Aqua session — a system
LaunchDaemon cannot reach `NSPasteboard`. `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` keep it up
across logins and console reconnects. Unload with
`launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/org.qsm.desktop-agent`.
